        "difficulty": "easy",
        "description": "<p>You have just joined your friend Neha, a data analyst at BookBarn, an online bookstore. Management needs an author-level sales summary for the upcoming quarterly review meeting. With thousands of purchases recorded daily in separate systems, manual aggregation is impossible. Your query will link book catalog details with sales transactions to reveal top-performing authors, helping the team optimize inventory and negotiate better contracts.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The &lsquo;books&rsquo; table has &lsquo;book_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;title&rsquo;, &lsquo;author&rsquo;, &lsquo;category&rsquo;, and &lsquo;price&rsquo;. &lsquo;book_id&rsquo; is primary key; each row describes a book with its genre and unit price.</li>\n<li>The &lsquo;orders&rsquo; table has &lsquo;order_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;book_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;quantity&rsquo;, and &lsquo;order_date&rsquo;. &lsquo;order_id&rsquo; is primary key; &lsquo;book_id&rsquo; references &lsquo;books&rsquo;, and each row logs copies sold on a specific date.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your task is to calculate, the number of copies sold and the total revenue generated each author.</p>\n<p>The output columns should be &lsquo;author&rsquo;, &lsquo;total_books_sold&rsquo;, and &lsquo;total_revenue&rsquo;, ordered by &lsquo;total_revenue&rsquo; in descending order, and then by &lsquo;author&rsquo; in ascending order.</p>",
