        "title": "Who’s the Boss",
        "difficulty": "easy",
        "description": "<p class=\"\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"564\">You&rsquo;ve been asked by your friend Ria, an HR analyst at TechCorp, to produce an employee hierarchy report. TechCorp wants insight into how each team member&rsquo;s compensation compares with their direct manager and how they rank within their department. With hundreds of employees and multiple management layers, manual calculation is impractical. By relating employees to their managers and applying department-wide comparisons, you will reveal salary gaps and identify top earners in each team. Your analysis will support performance reviews and compensation planning.</p>\n<ul data-start=\"566\" data-end=\"794\">\n<li class=\"\" data-start=\"566\" data-end=\"794\">\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"568\" data-end=\"794\">The &lsquo;employees&rsquo; table contains the columns &lsquo;emp_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;name&rsquo;, &lsquo;department&rsquo;, &lsquo;manager_id&rsquo;, and &lsquo;salary&rsquo;. &lsquo;emp_id&rsquo; is primary key; &lsquo;manager_id&rsquo; references another &lsquo;emp_id&rsquo;, and each row describes an employee&rsquo;s department and pay.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"796\" data-end=\"969\">Your task is to calculate, for each employee, their manager&rsquo;s salary, the salary difference between them and their manager, and their rank within their department by salary.</p>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"971\" data-end=\"1175\">The output columns should be &lsquo;employee_name&rsquo;, &lsquo;manager_salary&rsquo;, &lsquo;salary_difference&rsquo;, and &lsquo;department_rank&rsquo;, ordered by &lsquo;department_rank&rsquo; in ascending order, and then by &lsquo;employee_name&rsquo; in ascending order.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE employees (\n  emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(100),\n  department VARCHAR(50),\n  manager_id INT,\n  salary DECIMAL(10,2)\n);\nINSERT INTO employees VALUES\n(1, 'Alice', 'Engineering', NULL, 120000.00),\n(2, 'Bob', 'Engineering', 1, 95000.00),\n(3, 'Carol', 'Engineering', 1, 98000.00),\n(4, 'Dave', 'HR', NULL, 110000.00),\n(5, 'Eve', 'HR', 4, 90000.00);",
