        "id": "569895",
        "title": "Binge Analytics",
        "difficulty": "easy",
        "description": "<p class=\"\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"432\">You and your friend Rajiv, a content strategist at StreamWave, need to analyze episode-to-episode engagement trends across multiple web series. Raw viewership logs provide total views per episode, but management wants insight into whether audiences are growing or dropping off episodically. By relating each episode to its predecessor and summarizing differences, you&rsquo;ll pinpoint series with rising or declining audience engagement.</p>\n<ul data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"730\">\n<li class=\"\" data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"730\">\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"436\" data-end=\"730\">The &lsquo;episodes&rsquo; table contains the columns &lsquo;episode_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;series_name&rsquo;, &lsquo;season&rsquo;, &lsquo;episode_number&rsquo;, &lsquo;views&rsquo;, and &lsquo;prev_episode_id&rsquo;. &lsquo;episode_id&rsquo; is primary key; &lsquo;prev_episode_id&rsquo; references another &lsquo;episode_id&rsquo;, and each row describes an episode&rsquo;s metrics and its predecessor in the same series.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"732\" data-end=\"838\">Your task is to calculate, for each series, the average change in view count from one episode to the next.</p>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"840\" data-end=\"951\">The output columns should be &lsquo;series_name&rsquo; and &lsquo;avg_view_diff&rsquo;, ordered by &lsquo;avg_view_diff&rsquo; in descending order.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE episodes (\n  episode_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  series_name VARCHAR(100),\n  season INT,\n  episode_number INT,\n  views INT,\n  prev_episode_id INT\n);\nINSERT INTO episodes VALUES\n  (101, 'Gamma Tales', 1, 1, 1500, NULL),\n  (102, 'Gamma Tales', 1, 2, 1800, 101),\n  (103, 'Gamma Tales', 1, 3, 1600, 102),\n  (104, 'Delta Saga', 1, 1, 3000, NULL),\n  (105, 'Delta Saga', 1, 2, 2800, 104);",
        "slug": "binge-analytics",
        "originalCategory": "joins",
