        "title": "Pizza Time",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "description": "<p>Welcome to Slice &amp; Dice Pizzeria, where cheesy delights meet speedy delivery! As the new operations manager, you've got a hot mission: optimize our delivery system by calculating exact order times and fair delivery fees. Our hungry customers want transparency&mdash;they deserve to know exactly how long their Margherita masterpiece will take and what it'll cost to get it piping hot to their doorstep!&nbsp;</p>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"23\" data-end=\"93\">You are given three tables: 'pizzas', 'locations', and 'orders'.</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"97\" data-end=\"289\">The 'pizzas' table contains the columns 'pizza_id', 'pizza_name', 'preparation_time', and 'price'. Each row represents a pizza and includes how long it takes to prepare and its cost.</li>\n<li data-start=\"292\" data-end=\"473\">The 'locations' table contains the columns 'location_id', 'street_name', and 'delivery_time'. Each row represents a delivery location and the time it takes to deliver there.</li>\n<li data-start=\"476\" data-end=\"683\">The 'orders' table contains the columns 'order_id', 'customer_name', 'pizza_id', and 'street_name'. Each row represents an order made by a customer for a specific pizza to be delivered to a street.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"685\" data-end=\"701\">Your task is to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"704\" data-end=\"832\">Calculate the 'total_time' for each order as the sum of the pizza&rsquo;s 'preparation_time' and the location&rsquo;s 'delivery_time'.</li>\n<li data-start=\"835\" data-end=\"1040\">Calculate the 'total_paid' for each order. This is the pizza&rsquo;s base 'price', plus an additional delivery surcharge: ₹30 for every 15 minutes beyond the first 30 minutes of delivery time. Therefore,\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"141\">For first 30 minutes &rarr; ₹0</li>\n<li data-start=\"146\" data-end=\"168\">31&ndash;45 mins &rarr; ₹30</li>\n<li data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"195\">46&ndash;60 mins &rarr; ₹60, and so on</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"1042\" data-end=\"1179\">The final output should include the columns 'order_id', 'customer_name', 'total_time', and 'total_paid', ordered by 'order_id'.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE pizzas (\n    pizza_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    pizza_name VARCHAR(50),\n    preparation_time INT, \n    price INT \n);\n\nCREATE TABLE locations (\n    location_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    street_name VARCHAR(100),\n    delivery_time INT\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    order_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_name VARCHAR(100),\n    pizza_id INT,\n    street_name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n\nINSERT INTO pizzas VALUES \n(1, 'Margherita', 15, 250),\n(2, 'Farmhouse', 20, 300),\n(3, 'Pepperoni', 18, 350);\n\nINSERT INTO locations VALUES \n(1, 'MG Road', 25),\n(2, 'Baker Street', 35),\n(3, 'Linking Road', 10);\n\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES \n(1, 'Ravi', 1, 'MG Road'),\n(2, 'Anita', 2, 'Baker Street'),\n(3, 'Suresh', 3, 'Linking Road');",
