        "id": "569979",
        "title": "Lunar Love Connections",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "description": "<p class=\"\" data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"227\">LunarNet's colony director paced nervously in the low-gravity recreation dome. \"Our community bonding events keep failing,\" she lamented. \"We're pairing complete strangers who have nothing in common!\" As the newly hired data specialist, you realized the solution was hidden in the Moonbase social app's connection patterns.</p>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"229\" data-end=\"277\">You are given two tables: &lsquo;users&rsquo; and &lsquo;follows&rsquo;.</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"396\">The &lsquo;users&rsquo; table contains &lsquo;user_id&rsquo; and &lsquo;name&rsquo;. &lsquo;user_id&rsquo; is primary key; each row identifies a colony resident.</li>\n<li data-start=\"399\" data-end=\"538\">The &lsquo;follows&rsquo; table contains &lsquo;follower_id&rsquo; and &lsquo;followee_id&rsquo;. Both reference &lsquo;user_id&rsquo;, and each row records that one user follows another.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"540\" data-end=\"635\">Your task is to identify every unique pair of users who follow at least two of the same people. To avoid duplicates user1.id &gt; userd2.id</p>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"637\" data-end=\"792\">The output columns should be &lsquo;user1&rsquo;, &lsquo;user2&rsquo;, and &lsquo;common_count&rsquo;, ordered by &lsquo;common_count&rsquo; descending, then by &lsquo;user1&rsquo; ascending, then &lsquo;user2&rsquo; ascending.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE users (\n  user_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(100)\n);\nCREATE TABLE follows (\n  follower_id INT,\n  followee_id INT\n);\nINSERT INTO users VALUES\n  (1, 'Alice'),\n  (2, 'Bob'),\n  (3, 'Carol'),\n  (4, 'Dave'),\n  (5, 'Eve'),\n  (6, 'Frank');\nINSERT INTO follows VALUES\n  (1, 3), (1, 4), (1, 5),\n  (2, 3), (2, 4), (2, 6),\n  (3, 4), (3, 5),\n  (4, 5),\n  (5, 6);",
