        "id": "570200",
        "title": "Class Chain",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "description": "<p>In the Twilight Dance Loft, the manager wants to link each dancer with the very next person scheduled for class&mdash;and only spotlight those pairs who&rsquo;ve both dug into the library&rsquo;s collection&mdash;so they can form reading&ndash;dance buddies.</p>\n<p>You are given two tables: &lsquo;dancers&rsquo; and &lsquo;loans&rsquo;.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The &lsquo;dancers&rsquo; table contains &lsquo;dancer_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;name&rsquo;, and &lsquo;class_date&rsquo;. Each row notes a dancer&rsquo;s scheduled class date.</li>\n<li>The &lsquo;loans&rsquo; table contains &lsquo;loan_id&rsquo;, &lsquo;dancer_id&rsquo;, and &lsquo;book_title&rsquo;. Each row logs when a dancer borrowed a book.</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"432\" data-end=\"448\">Your task is to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"451\" data-end=\"515\">Consider only those dancers who have borrowed at least one book.</li>\n<li data-start=\"518\" data-end=\"629\">For each dancer, find the first dancer (after them chronologically by 'class_date') who also borrowed a book.</li>\n<li data-start=\"632\" data-end=\"701\">Return pairs of such dancers showing the earlier and the next dancer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include columns 'dancer1', 'dancer2', and 'class_date2', sorted by 'class_date2' in ascending order. Limit the results to the first 5 rows.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE dancers (\n  dancer_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(100),\n  class_date DATE\n);\nCREATE TABLE loans (\n  loan_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  dancer_id INT,\n  book_title VARCHAR(200)\n);\nINSERT INTO dancers VALUES\n  (101, 'Finn',     '2025-06-02'),\n  (102, 'Giselle',  '2025-06-04'),\n  (103, 'Harper',   '2025-06-06'),\n  (104, 'Ianthe',   '2025-06-08'),\n  (105, 'Jax',      '2025-06-10');\nINSERT INTO loans VALUES\n  (101, 101, 'Pirouette Primer'),\n  (102, 103, 'Rhythm Guide'),\n  (103, 104, 'Ballet Biographies'),\n  (104, 105, 'Modern Moves');\n\n",
