        "title": "Panicked Pratik",
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "description": "<p>Pratik, a bright-eyed intern at Maze Inc., arrived on his first day buzzing with excitement. But his nerves spiked when his manager handed him a task to organize project timelines. He needs to group tasks for each project into 3 categories based on their due dates relative to the project&rsquo;s start date. He is given two tables: 'projects' and 'tasks'. &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'projects' table includes the columns 'id', 'project_name', and 'start_date'. Each row represents a project with its unique ID and the date it started. &nbsp;</li>\n<li>The 'tasks' table includes the columns 'id', 'project_id', 'task_name', and 'due_date'. Each task is associated with a project through the 'project_id'. &nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Overwhelmed by the projects and tasks tables, Pratik&rsquo;s confidence crumbled. Panicking, he turned to you&mdash;his senior and friend at the company&mdash;and asked for help with your SQL skills. You stepped in and guided him to: &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Categorize the tasks of each project based on how many days after the 'start_date' the task 'due_date' falls:\n<ul>\n<li>If the tasks due 0&ndash;7 days (inclusive) from the start date belong to week_1_tasks.</li>\n<li>If the tasks due 8&ndash;13 days (inclusive) from the start date belong to week_2_tasks.</li>\n<li>If the tasks due 14 days or later from the start date belong to week_3_and_further_tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>For each project, list the task names in each week group, sorted by 'due_date' and then by 'task_id', joined with ' -&gt; '.</li>\n<li>If there are no tasks in a particular week group for a project, return 'No tasks' for that group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include columns 'project_name', 'week_1_tasks', 'week_2_tasks', and 'week_3_and_further_tasks', ordered by 'project_name'.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE projects (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    project_name VARCHAR(100),\n    start_date DATE\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE tasks (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    project_id INT,\n    task_name VARCHAR(100),\n    due_date DATE,\n    FOREIGN KEY (project_id) REFERENCES projects(id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO projects (id, project_name, start_date) VALUES\n(1, 'App', '2025-07-01'),\n(2, 'ML', '2025-07-05'),\n(3, 'Website', '2025-07-10');\n           \nINSERT INTO tasks (id, project_id, task_name, due_date) VALUES\n(1, 1, 'Design', '2025-07-02'),       \n(2, 1, 'Build', '2025-07-04'),      \n(3, 1, 'Test', '2025-07-09'),         \n(4, 1, 'Release', '2025-07-15'),      \n(5, 3, 'Draft', '2025-07-11'),       \n(6, 3, 'Develop', '2025-07-17'),          \n(7, 3, 'QA', '2025-07-18');  ",
