        "description": "<p class=\"\" data-start=\"23\" data-end=\"85\">Your programmer friend Arjun just made a dangerous bet with his know-it-all chemist roommate Karan. The challenge? Arjun has until midnight to calculate bond energies for Karan's chemical compounds. If he succeeds, he gets the coveted window-side of their shared room for a month &ndash; crucial because that's where the WiFi signal is strongest. If he fails, he's stuck in the WiFi dead zone. Karan smugly provides two tables: bonding_energy and compounds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"89\" data-end=\"393\">The 'bonding_energy' table contains three columns: 'element_1', 'element_2', and 'energy'. Each row represents the energy required to bond two elements. The combination of 'element_1' and 'element_2' is unique in table.</li>\n<li data-start=\"89\" data-end=\"393\">The 'compounds' table includes the columns 'compound_id', 'compound_name', and 'compound_formulae'. Each row represents a compound, its associated ID, name, and chemical formula.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Element format: elements can only contain either strings of one character (such as 'H', 'O', etc.) or two characters (such as 'Na', 'He', etc.), where the first character is always uppercase and the second character, if present, is always lowercase.</p>\n<p>Compound formulae are structured as hyphen-delimited strings where each hyphen represents a chemical bond between adjacent elements. The string must contain no leading/trailing hyphens or spaces. Terminal elements bond with one neighbor (e.g., the H in H-O-H), while internal elements bond with two (e.g., the O in H-O-H). Each hyphen separates exactly one bond pair, and formulae must contain at least two elements (e.g., \"C-O\" valid, \"C\" invalid).</p>\n<p>Note: Bonding energy of same element pair (e.g. H-H) is always zero.</p>\n<p>Arjun knows this can be solved with SQL, but he&rsquo;s drawing a blank. In a panic, he turns to you: &ldquo;You&rsquo;ve got to help me crush Karan&rsquo;s ego with SQL!&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Your task is to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"744\" data-end=\"859\">Extract all adjacent pairs of elements in each compound formula (i.e., from 'A-B-C' extract 'A-B' and 'B-C').</li>\n<li data-start=\"862\" data-end=\"982\">For each pair, determine the bond energy by matching the elements (regardless of order) to the 'bonding_energy' table.</li>\n<li data-start=\"985\" data-end=\"1038\">Calculate the total bonding energy for each compound.</li>\n<li data-start=\"1041\" data-end=\"1103\">Return the 'compound_id', 'compound_name', and 'total_energy' for all compounds with a positive bonding energy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include columns 'compound_id', 'compound_name', and 'total_energy', ordered by 'compound_id'.</p>",
        "schema": "-- Table Definitions\nCREATE TABLE bonding_energy (\n    element_1 VARCHAR(2),\n    element_2 VARCHAR(2),\n    energy INT,\n    PRIMARY KEY (element_1, element_2)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE compounds (\n    compound_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    compound_name VARCHAR(50),\n    compound_formulae VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n-- Sample Data\nINSERT INTO bonding_energy VALUES\n('C', 'O', 40),\n('H', 'O', 30),\n('C', 'H', 25),\n('Cl', 'Na', 45);\n\nINSERT INTO compounds VALUES\n(1, 'Carbon Dioxide', 'O-C-O'),\n(2, 'Water', 'H-O-H'),\n(3, 'Methane', 'H-C-H-H-H'),\n(4, 'Table Salt', 'Na-Cl');",
        "slug": "two-roommates-bond",
