        "id": "571957",
        "title": "Coral Constructs",
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "description": "<p data-start=\"91\" data-end=\"389\">You are given two tables: 'modules' and 'inspections'. The 'modules' table represents a hierarchical structure of construction modules, where each module may have a parent module. The 'inspections' table tracks inspection events for each module, along with their status and inspection date.</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"500\">The 'modules' table includes the columns 'module_id', 'name', 'parent_id', and 'completion_date'.</li>\n<li data-start=\"503\" data-end=\"620\">The 'inspections' table includes the columns 'inspection_id', 'module_id', 'inspection_date', and 'status'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"622\" data-end=\"972\">Write a query to identify the most recently passed modules (based on the latest inspection marked as <code data-start=\"723\" data-end=\"731\">'Pass'</code>). From these, identify their chain in the module hierarchy starting from the top-level parent, and calculate the depth of each module in this chain. Also, determine the root module (the highest ancestor in the chain) for each passed module.</p>\n<p data-start=\"974\" data-end=\"1000\">For each qualified module:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"1003\" data-end=\"1094\">Extract the module type by taking the prefix of the 'name' before the underscore (<code data-start=\"1087\" data-end=\"1092\">'_'</code>).</li>\n<li data-start=\"1097\" data-end=\"1220\">Include the module's name, its root module&rsquo;s name, the depth in its hierarchy, and the date of its last passing inspection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include the columns 'module_type', 'module_name', 'root_module', 'depth', and 'last_inspection', sorted by 'last_inspection' in descending order and 'module_name' in ascending order.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE modules (\n  module_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(100),\n  parent_id INT,\n  completion_date DATE\n);\nCREATE TABLE inspections (\n  inspection_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  module_id INT,\n  inspection_date DATE,\n  status VARCHAR(10)\n);\nINSERT INTO modules VALUES\n  (1, 'Core_A',      NULL,        '2025-01-10'),\n  (2, 'Habitat_B1',   1,          '2025-02-05'),\n  (3, 'Habitat_B2',   1,          '2025-02-10'),\n  (4, 'Lab_C1',       2,          '2025-03-01'),\n  (5, 'Lab_C2',       3,          '2025-03-05'),\n  (6, 'Dock_D1',      2,          '2025-04-01'),\n  (7, 'Dock_D2',      5,          '2025-04-10'),\n  (8, 'Tech_E1',      4,          '2025-04-15'),\n  (9, 'Tech_E2',      5,          '2025-04-20'),\n  (10,'Habitat_B3',   1,          '2025-05-01');\nINSERT INTO inspections VALUES\n  (1,  1, '2025-02-01', 'Pass'),\n  (2,  2, '2025-02-20', 'Fail'),\n  (3,  2, '2025-03-10', 'Pass'),\n  (4,  3, '2025-03-15', 'Pass'),\n  (5,  4, '2025-04-05', 'Pass'),\n  (6,  5, '2025-04-12', 'Fail'),\n  (7,  5, '2025-04-18', 'Pass'),\n  (8,  6, '2025-04-08', 'Pass'),\n  (9,  7, '2025-04-15', 'Fail'),\n  (10, 7, '2025-04-22', 'Pass'),\n  (11, 8, '2025-04-20', 'Pass'),\n  (12, 9, '2025-04-25', 'Pass'),\n  (13,10, '2025-05-10', 'Fail');\n\n",
